    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d207">

                <pb facs="d206-02" n="2" sameAs="frus:frus1969-76ve05p1/d206-02"/>

                <head>207. Telegram 74799 From the Department of State to
                    the Embassy in Nigeria<anchor n="1" xml:id="d207-f1"/>
                    <anchor n="2" xml:id="d207-f2"/>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1971-04-30"
                        >April 30, 1971</date>
                </dateline>
                <pb facs="d207-01" n="1" xml:id="d207-01"/>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>SUBJ:</label>
                    <item>FMS Program</item>
                </list>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>REF:</label>
                    <item>Lagos 3804</item>
                </list>
                <p>1. You are authorized to inform FMG of
                    Presidential Determination finding Nigeria eligible to purchase defense articles
                    and defense services under FMS Act.</p>
                <p>2. While PD also authorizes sale of military equipment to Nigeria, we believe the
                        FMG should be encouraged to focus primarily
                    on purchase of training. Legislative restrictions regarding the supply of
                    defense articles to Africa, the current $40 million ceiling on USG provision
                    approaching of arms to Africa (which we are already for FY 71), and general
                    Congressional wariness on the matter of arms for Nigeria as aftermath of the
                    civil war experience dictate that we move cautiously on any prospective military
                    materiel sales program for Nigeria.</p>
                <closer><signed>Irwin</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d207-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d207-f1-content">
                <p>Source: National Archives, RG 59, Central Files 1970–73, DEF 12–5 Nigeria.
                    Confidential. Also sent to Lagos. Repeated to CINCSTRIKE. Drafted by Kormann
                    (AF/I) and Critz (AF/I); cleared in AF/NI, DOD/ISA, Joint Staff, DOD/ISA/MA&amp;S, AF/I and PM/MAS; and
                    approved in AF/I.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d207-f2" n="2" xml:id="d207-f2-content">
                <p>This joint Department of State-Department of Defense telegram informed the
                    Embassy that it was authorized to inform the Federal Military Government (FMG) that Nigeria was eligible to purchase defense
                    articles and services under the Foreign Military Sales Act, but the FMG should be encouraged to focus primarily on the
                    purchase of training.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
